## Derivation of the first-order Euler-Lagrange equations on $J^1(X,Y)$

### 1. Setup and notation

Let $\pi_{XY}:Y\to X$ be a fiber bundle with $\dim X = n$, $\operatorname{rank} Y = m$. Local coordinates on $X$: $(x^\alpha)$, $\alpha=1,\dots,n$. Adapted coordinates on $Y$: $(x^\alpha,y^i)$, $i=1,\dots,m$. The first jet manifold $J^1(X,Y)$ carries adapted coordinates $(x^\alpha,y^i,y^i_\alpha)$, where $y^i_\alpha$ represents $\partial y^i/\partial x^\alpha$.

The Lagrangian density is
$$L = \mathcal{L}(x^\alpha,y^i,y^i_\alpha)\,\omega,\qquad \omega = dx^1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx^n.$$

A section $\sigma:X\to Y$ (i.e. $\pi_{XY}\circ\sigma=\operatorname{id}_X$) has first jet prolongation
$$j^1\sigma:X\to J^1(X,Y),\qquad (y^i_\alpha\circ j^1\sigma)(x)=\partial_\alpha\sigma^i(x).$$

A section of $J^1(X,Y)\to X$ is *holonomic* iff it is of the form $j^1\sigma$ for some $\sigma:X\to Y$. The variational principle is imposed over holonomic sections, so the unknown is really $\sigma$, and the action functional is
$$S[\sigma]=\int_X (j^1\sigma)^*L=\int_X \mathcal{L}\bigl(x,\sigma^i(x),\partial_\alpha\sigma^i(x)\bigr)\,d^nx.$$

### 2. Variations

Let $\sigma_\varepsilon$ be a smooth one-parameter family of sections with $\sigma_0=\sigma$, defined for $\varepsilon$ near $0$. The *variation field* (vertical vector field along $\sigma$) is
$$v^i(x)=\left.\frac{d}{d\varepsilon}\right|_{\varepsilon=0}\sigma_\varepsilon^i(x),\qquad v=\left.\frac{d}{d\varepsilon}\right|_{0}j^1\sigma_\varepsilon = v^i\frac{\partial}{\partial y^i}+(\partial_\alpha v^i)\frac{\partial}{\partial y^i_\alpha}.$$

We take $v$ compactly supported in the interior of $X$ (so $v$ and all its derivatives vanish on $\partial X$, or $X$ is taken without boundary).

### 3. First variation of the action

Differentiating under the integral,
$$\left.\frac{dS[\sigma_\varepsilon]}{d\varepsilon}\right|_{0}=\int_X\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}\,v^i+\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\,\partial_\alpha v^i\right]\omega,$$
where $\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial y^i$ and $\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial y^i_\alpha$ are evaluated at $j^1\sigma(x)$. (Einstein summation over $\alpha$ and $i$.)

### 4. Integration by parts (the divergence theorem on $X$)

For the second term, use the identity on the $n$-form
$$\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\,\partial_\alpha v^i\,\omega = d\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\,v^i\,i_{\partial_\alpha}\omega\right) + v^i\,\partial_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)\!\omega\cdot(-1),$$
more compactly: in coordinates with $\omega=d^nx$,
$$\int_X \frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\,\partial_\alpha v^i\,d^nx = \int_{\partial X}\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\,v^i\,d x^1\wedge\cdots\widehat{dx^\alpha}\cdots\wedge dx^n \;-\;\int_X \partial_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right) v^i\,d^nx.$$

The boundary integral vanishes because $v$ has compact support in $\operatorname{int}X$. Hence
$$\delta S[\sigma]\cdot v = \int_X\left[\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-\partial_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)\right]v^i\,\omega,$$
where $\partial_\alpha(\cdot)$ denotes the *total derivative* along the section $\sigma$ (see §5).

### 5. The total derivative

Along $\sigma$, the function $\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial y^i_\alpha$ is a function of $(x,\sigma^i(x),\partial_\beta\sigma^i(x))$. Its total $x^\alpha$-derivative is
$$D_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)=\frac{\partial}{\partial x^\alpha}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)+\frac{\partial}{\partial y^j}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)y^j_\alpha+\frac{\partial}{\partial y^j_\beta}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)y^j_{\beta\alpha},$$
evaluated at $j^1\sigma$ (so $y^j_\alpha=\partial_\alpha\sigma^j$, $y^j_{\beta\alpha}=\partial_\beta\partial_\alpha\sigma^j$). The operator $D_\alpha$ is the formal total derivative on $J^1$ (prolonged to $J^2$ when acting on $y^i_\alpha$-dependent functions).

### 6. Fundamental lemma and the Euler-Lagrange equations

By the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations (the integrand is continuous and $v^i$ is an arbitrary compactly supported vertical field), $\delta S[\sigma]\cdot v=0$ for all such $v$ iff
$$\boxed{\;\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-D_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)=0,\qquad i=1,\dots,m\;}$$
with summation over $\alpha=1,\dots,n$. In fully expanded form, evaluated along $j^1\sigma$:
$$\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-\frac{\partial}{\partial x^\alpha}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)-\frac{\partial}{\partial y^j}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)y^j_\alpha-\frac{\partial}{\partial y^j_\beta}\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)y^j_{\beta\alpha}=0.$$

### 7. Coordinate-free form (Poincaré-Cartan / Euler-Lagrange form)

The same equations are encoded by the *Euler-Lagrange form*
$$\mathcal{E}(L)=\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-D_\alpha\!\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)dy^i\wedge\omega\in\Omega^{n+1}_{J^1}(Y),$$
a contact-horizontal $(n+1)$-form on $J^1(X,Y)$. A holonomic section $j^1\sigma$ is *extremal* (a stationary point of $S$) iff
$$(j^1\sigma)^*\,i_\xi\,\mathcal{E}(L)=0\quad\text{for every vertical vector field }\xi\text{ on }Y\to X,$$
equivalently $(j^1\sigma)^*\mathcal{E}(L)=0$ on vertical contractions, which reproduces exactly the boxed equations above.

### 8. Summary

The first-order Euler-Lagrange equations for sections $\sigma:X\to Y$ (equivalently, for holonomic sections $j^1\sigma:X\to J^1(X,Y)$) of the Lagrangian density $L=\mathcal{L}(x^\alpha,y^i,y^i_\alpha)\,\omega$ are
$$\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-D_\alpha\!\left(\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i_\alpha}\right)=0,\qquad i=1,\dots,m,$$
i.e. in expanded form
$$\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial y^i}-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{y^i_\alpha}}{\partial x^\alpha}-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{y^i_\alpha}}{\partial y^j}\,y^j_\alpha-\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}_{y^i_\alpha}}{\partial y^j_\beta}\,y^j_{\beta\alpha}=0,$$
with $\mathcal{L}_{y^i_\alpha}:=\partial\mathcal{L}/\partial y^i_\alpha$ and summation over repeated indices $\alpha,\beta,j$. These are $m$ second-order PDEs for the $m$ unknown functions $\sigma^i(x^\alpha)$, obtained by the standard variational argument: write the action, take a vertical variation with compact support, integrate the term involving $\partial_\alpha v^i$ by parts (boundary term vanishes), and apply the fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations.

